The '''Four House Era''' (1709–1743) was a period in the history of the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] characterised by the operation of the four-house parliament, the rising power of the merchant class, and the eventual transition from absolutist to constitutional monarchy. The period began with the creation of the parliament under [[Iacob V]] following the [[Burawa Imperial Split|Imperial Split]] of 1709 and concluded with the adoption of the [[Constitution of 1743]], which established a limited monarchy under the young king [[Nuur V]].

The era was marked by parliamentary gridlock, the [[First War of the Isthmus]] (1711–1716) which pitted [[Illypnia]]n powers against [[Fosia]]n colonisers, the [[Restoration Revolution]] (1741–1742) in which a military commander attempted to restore Galwa rule, and the assassination of Iacob V by his own guards. The chaotic final years of the period produced a constitutional settlement that fundamentally altered the Burawi political order.

== Background ==

{{main|Burawa Imperial Split}}

The [[Burawa Imperial Split|Imperial Split]] of 1709 had divided the Burawi kingdom into two states: the eastern [[Kingdom of Burawa]] under [[Iacob V]] and the western [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]] under the child king [[Cali IV]] with his mother [[Sahra]] as regent. To secure the support of the merchant houses, the [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] aristocracy, and the Church during the succession crisis, Iacob V had been compelled to create a four-house parliament, a representative body that shared power with the crown.

The four houses were the House of the King, representing royal authority; the House of the Church, representing ecclesiastical interests; the House of the Hogas, representing the landed aristocracy; and the House of the Landowners, representing the merchant and planter elite. This arrangement was unprecedented in Burawi history. For centuries, the kingdom had been governed as an absolutist monarchy; the parliament represented a fundamental break with tradition. The question of how the new system would function, and whether it could function at all, defined the politics of the era.

== Reign of Iacob V (1709–1741) ==

[[Iacob V]], who had been an adult claimant with his own power base during the succession crisis, proved to be a capable administrator but one increasingly overwhelmed by the forces his reign had unleashed. The creation of the parliament had been a necessary concession to secure the throne; it was not an arrangement the king had sought, and throughout his reign he struggled to govern effectively within its constraints. Iacob V's instinct was to centralise authority and govern decisively. The parliament's instinct was to assert its prerogatives and constrain the crown. The resulting tension produced not creative compromise but paralysis.

The four-house parliament was, from its inception, a mechanism for deadlock. Each house represented distinct interests that frequently conflicted: the crown sought to maximise royal authority; the Church sought to protect ecclesiastical privileges and advance its moral agenda; the Hogas sought to regain the influence they had lost during the late colonial period; the Landowners, dominated by the merchant houses of [[House Guleed|Guleed]], [[House Warsame|Warsame]], and [[House Ciise|Ciise]], sought to advance commercial interests and constrain aristocratic privilege.

The parliament had been designed without a clear mechanism for resolving disputes between houses. When the houses disagreed, which was frequently, legislation stalled. The king could not force measures through; the houses could not override royal veto. The result was chronic gridlock. Critical reforms languished for years. Colonial administration required overhaul; the army and navy needed modernisation; the succession question remained unresolved. Parliament debated, quarrelled, and produced little. The merchants, meanwhile, accumulated wealth and influence through their control of colonial trade networks. Their economic leverage translated into political power that no parliamentary vote could fully capture.

The [[House Guleed|House of Guleed]] emerged during this period as the most powerful of the merchant houses. [[Xason Guleed]], who led the house through the middle decades of the century, exemplified the new type of political figure the era produced: not a Hoga lord with military retainers, nor a Church prelate with spiritual authority, but a commercial magnate whose wealth purchased influence at every level of the state. The Guleed family had made their fortune in the colonial trade, particularly in the triangle trade connecting [[Kaftia]], [[Abyala]], and the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] markets. Their ships carried goods, their financial networks extended throughout the colonial sphere, and their credit sustained royal finances when parliamentary deadlock prevented the crown from raising revenue through normal channels.

Xason Guleed understood that in a deadlocked system, the ability to offer or withhold financial support was itself a form of power. He cultivated relationships across the houses: loans to Hoga families whose agricultural incomes had declined; charitable donations to Church institutions; strategic investments in enterprises controlled by royal favourites. By the 1730s, the merchant class, and Guleed in particular, exercised influence disproportionate to their formal representation in parliament.

Relations between the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] and the western [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]] remained cold but stable throughout the era. The split of 1709 had created two states with competing claims to legitimacy; neither recognised the other's ruler as the rightful Burawi monarch, but neither sought to overturn the settlement by force.

The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] served as the primary intermediary between the two kingdoms. The [[Cardinal of Burawa]], seated at [[Weli Markos]], maintained ecclesiastical authority over both states. Church channels facilitated communication, mediated disputes, and preserved a sense of shared Burawi Catholic identity across the political divide. Low-level competition persisted. Both kingdoms claimed the right to speak for Burawi interests in international fora; both sought advantage in trade negotiations; both cultivated allies among the colonial protectorates. But open conflict was avoided, and the Church's mediating role helped prevent crises from escalating.

== First War of the Isthmus (1711–1716) ==

{{main|First War of the Isthmus}}

The [[First War of the Isthmus]] was the first major conflict between [[Illypnia]]n and [[Fosia]]n colonial powers in the New World. Fought between 1711 and 1716, it centred on control of [[San Cristóbal]], a port on the [[Balboa]] isthmus, the narrow land bridge connecting the continents of [[Abyala]] and [[Lurandia]].

The [[Zong|Kingdom of Zong]], the dominant maritime power of eastern [[Fosia]], had established the colony of [[Tamsui]] on the western coast of the New World. Zong's commercial interests required a reliable route to [[Illypnia]]n markets; the long sea voyage around the southern tip of Lurandia was costly and time-consuming. The Isthmus of Balboa offered a shortcut: control of the isthmus would allow Zong to transport goods overland between the western (Fosian-facing) and eastern (Illypnian-facing) coasts. San Cristóbal, a port on the eastern coast of the isthmus, was the key to this vision. The port was claimed by the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|Volisania]], which had established it as an outpost controlling access to the overland route. Zong determined to seize the port and thereby secure its trade connection to Illypnia.

In 1711, Zong launched an expedition to capture San Cristóbal. The attack represented a direct challenge to Illypnian colonial dominance in the region. [[Volisania]], the preeminent Illypnian naval power, responded with a full mobilisation of its forces. Burawa, bound to Volisania by the strategic alignment that had developed since the 1680s, committed fully to the war. The kingdom dispatched naval forces, ground troops, and financial resources to support the Illypnian coalition. [[Lacashe]], the Burawi protectorate in Abyala, likewise contributed forces as a Burawi ally.

The war quickly expanded beyond a local colonial skirmish. [[Breisland]] and [[Merica]], two Illypnian powers that had historically competed with Volisania for colonial advantage, chose to support Zong rather than their fellow Illypnians. The calculation was straightforward: a Volisanian defeat would weaken the kingdom that dominated Illypnian colonial politics, even if the immediate beneficiary was a Fosian power. The conflict thus assumed the character of a broader struggle between competing imperial systems. Illypnian powers fought on both sides; Fosian forces contested Illypnian colonial territory; colonial possessions changed hands or were ravaged in fighting.

Zong forces succeeded in blockading San Cristóbal in 1713 and eventually captured the port after a sustained siege. The Illypnian coalition, however, mounted a counter-offensive that surrounded the Zong expeditionary force. Cut off from reinforcement and supply, the Zong position became untenable. Rather than face destruction, Zong opened negotiations. The Illypnian coalition, which had suffered significant losses and faced the prospect of a prolonged campaign to retake the port, was amenable to a settlement.

{{main|Treaty of Remoria}}

The [[Treaty of Remoria]] (1716) ended the war. The terms represented a compromise, but one that reflected poorly on Volisania, the preeminent Illypnian power. Zong agreed to withdraw from San Cristóbal, but in exchange received docking and passage rights through Volisanian ports, access to the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] and Illypnian trade networks that Zong had sought all along.

For Volisania, the outcome was humiliating. The kingdom had mobilised its vast naval resources, committed to a major war, and emerged having granted its opponent the very concessions that war had been fought to prevent. The perception that Volisania had been outmanoeuvred at the negotiating table contributed to domestic unrest that would culminate in the [[Volisanian Civil War]] of 1736. For Burawa, the war had been costly in blood and treasure but produced no tangible gains. The kingdom had honoured its alliance with Volisania and demonstrated its commitment to the Illypnian coalition, but the alliance itself seemed increasingly questionable. The treaty's terms suggested that Volisania could not reliably protect Illypnian interests; the kingdom's subsequent internal crisis raised further doubts about its reliability as a partner.

== Restoration Revolution (1741–1742) ==

{{main|Restoration Revolution}}

The [[Restoration Revolution]] was a violent attempt to overthrow [[Iacob V]] and restore the unity of the Burawi kingdoms under a Galwa claimant. It failed, but its failure produced a political crisis that transformed the Burawi state.

[[Gali Darg]] was a military commander who had risen through the ranks of the Burawi army during the First War of the Isthmus. His record was distinguished; his following among the officer corps was substantial. In the late 1730s, Darg began to articulate a political programme: the reunification of the Burawi kingdoms under a single monarch. Darg claimed to have been in contact with the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa|Galwa court]] and to have received assurances of support for a restorationist uprising. He presented himself as the instrument of Galwa's legitimate claim to rule all Burawi lands. The Galwa government, however, denied any connection to Darg and refused to endorse his claims. Whether Darg had been genuinely assured of Galwa support, and whether the Galwa court had simply abandoned him when the uprising failed, remains a matter of historical dispute. What is clear is that Galwa provided no material assistance to the revolution.

In 1741, Darg launched his uprising. Forces loyal to him seized key military installations and moved against the capital at [[Samara]]. The revolution was violent from the outset; Darg's supporters and royalist forces clashed in the streets of several cities. Crucially, Darg had cultivated support within the royal guard. Guards who had been stationed to protect the king were, in fact, Darg's agents. In the chaos of the uprising, these guards assassinated Iacob V. The king was killed by the very men charged with his protection.

Despite the king's death, the revolution failed. Darg's forces were unable to secure control of the capital or to win over sufficient elements of the military establishment. The Hoga lords, the merchant houses, and the Church hierarchy all opposed the uprising, not out of loyalty to the dead king, but out of concern for their own positions in a Darg-led regime. Royalist forces regrouped and counter-attacked. Darg was captured, tried, and executed. The Restoration Revolution had lasted approximately one year and had produced one indisputable result: the death of the king.

Iacob V's death left the throne to his son, [[Nuur V]], a teenager of approximately fourteen or fifteen years. The young king was in no position to assert royal authority. The Hoga lords and the merchant landowners, who had cooperated to defeat Darg, now cooperated to constrain the monarchy permanently.

== Constitutional Convention (1742–1743) ==

In the aftermath of the Restoration Revolution, the great powers of the realm, the Hoga aristocracy and the landowning merchant class, convened to reshape the Burawi state. A constitutional convention was called, with delegates drawn from the Hoga families, the merchant houses, the Church hierarchy, and the remaining royalist elements.

Two figures emerged as the architects of the new order. [[Lord Hodan]], a reform-minded Hoga lord, represented the aristocracy's interest in a stable political order that would protect their remaining privileges while preventing future royal absolutism. [[Xason Guleed]], the merchant magnate, represented the commercial class's interest in a government that would facilitate trade and protect property rights. [[Cardinal Petros III]], the head of the [[Burawi Catholic Church]], served as mediator. The Church had emerged from the Restoration Revolution with its position intact; it had neither supported Darg nor been implicated in the royal assassination. The Cardinal's neutrality made him an acceptable arbiter among the competing factions.

The [[Constitution of 1743]] established a limited monarchy with parliamentary supremacy at its core. The key provisions included parliamentary supremacy, with the four-house parliament confirmed as the supreme legislative body; the crown could no longer govern without parliamentary consent, and royal decrees required parliamentary ratification. Aristocratic privileges were granted formal protection, with the Hoga families receiving guarantees for their landholdings and traditional rights, and the Hoga house of parliament receiving enhanced powers over matters of inheritance, land tenure, and local administration. Church-state relations were enshrined in the constitution, with the special position of the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] confirmed and the Church retaining its role in education, marriage, and moral regulation. The Cardinal continued to serve as mediator between the Burawi kingdoms. Royal succession was confirmed in the Kadim-Kaysoor line, with Nuur V recognised as legitimate king, but the young king's powers were strictly limited. A regency council drawn from parliament would govern until he reached his majority.

During the constitutional convention, a parliamentary interregnum governed the kingdom. The four houses, working in unusual harmony, oversaw the transition and ratified the new constitutional order. When the constitution was adopted in 1743, Nuur V formally accepted its terms, becoming the first constitutional monarch in Burawi history. The Four House Era was at an end. The [[Constitutional Era of Burawa|Constitutional Era]] had begun.

== Legacy ==

The Four House Era was a period of political experimentation and upheaval. The four-house parliament, created as a concession to secure Iacob V's accession, proved unwieldy and prone to deadlock. Yet it also established the principle that the king could not rule without the consent of the realm's major interests: the Church, the aristocracy, and the merchant class.

The era demonstrated the rising power of the merchant houses. Through economic leverage rather than military force, figures such as Xason Guleed accumulated influence that rivalled or exceeded that of traditional Hoga lords. The Constitution of 1743 recognised this shift by enshrining both aristocratic and commercial interests in the fundamental law. The Restoration Revolution, though it failed, revealed the fragility of the post-split political order. A single military commander, with limited support from the Galwa court, had nearly toppled the kingdom and had succeeded in assassinating the king. The constitution was, in part, a response to this demonstration of vulnerability: by constraining the monarchy and empowering parliament, the great interests of the realm sought to create a more stable and more defensible political system.

The Church's role as mediator, both between the two Burawi kingdoms and among the factions within the eastern kingdom, was confirmed and formalised. The Cardinal of Burawa, seated at neutral Weli Markos, became a fixture of Burawi political life, an institution that transcended the division of 1709 and the constitutional transformation of 1743.

